Rem ==================================================================
Rem str.shoddy — Shoddy standard library: strings
Rem Built on the eighteen string builtins, each documented one by one
Rem immediately below: & Len Left Right Mid Instr InstrFrom Upper Lower
Rem Chr Asc Codes FromCodes CodeAt Str Val ValOr IsNumeric. Pure.
Rem Three families of its own: surgery (Split .. StrRep), boolean
Rem conversions (IsBool / ToBool / ToBoolOr over the primitive
Rem ToBoolOpt — the string-to-number side lives in the IsNumeric / Val /
Rem ValOr builtins), and fixed-width formatting (PadLeft / PadRight /
Rem PadZero / ToFixed / Commas).
Rem ==================================================================

Rem ---- the builtins this machine is built on -------------------------
Rem
Rem NOT Defs. The engine dispatches these, and a Def whose name is a
Rem builtin is refused; they are written here because str.shoddy is
Rem where a reader goes looking for them. The flat reference is section
Rem 10 of docs/spec.html, and the stack effects below are the ones the
Rem compiler's own Effects.cs table declares.
Rem
Rem Positions and lengths count from 1, and every one of these is pure.

Rem s1 & s2 As String                                 ( s1 s2 -- s )
Rem     The two strings joined. The one string word with no name — it is
Rem     an operator, and it adds numbers too.

Rem Len(s As String) As Number                        ( s -- n )
Rem     How many characters the string holds. Length is the LIST word and
Rem     refuses a string outright, so the two are not a near miss.

Rem Left(s As String, n As Number) As String          ( s n -- s2 )
Rem     The first n characters, or the whole string if it is shorter.
Rem     A fractional n truncates rather than refusing.

Rem Right(s As String, n As Number) As String         ( s n -- s2 )
Rem     The last n characters, or the whole string if it is shorter.

Rem Mid(s As String, start As Number, n As Number) As String
Rem                                                   ( s start n -- s2 )
Rem     n characters from position start. A range past the end is trimmed
Rem     to what is there rather than refused, which is what makes CodeAt
Rem     worth having.

Rem Instr(s As String, sub As String) As Number       ( s sub -- pos )
Rem     Where sub first appears in s, or 0 when it does not. An empty sub
Rem     answers 0.

Rem InstrFrom(s As String, sub As String, from As Number) As Number
Rem                                                   ( s sub from -- pos )
Rem     The same search begun at position from, without copying the
Rem     subject to get there. Split and Replace below are linear in their
Rem     subject because of this word; a from past the end answers 0.

Rem Upper(s As String) As String                      ( s -- s2 )
Rem     The string case-folded up. The language folds word names the same
Rem     way, which is why reckoner.shoddy calls this at every lookup.

Rem Lower(s As String) As String                      ( s -- s2 )
Rem     The string case-folded down.

Rem Chr(n As Number) As String                        ( n -- s )
Rem     The character with code n. Chr(0) is the EMPTY STRING and not a
Rem     NUL — FromCodes is how a NUL is built. A code above 65535 casts
Rem     to 16 bits and wraps silently rather than failing.

Rem Asc(s As String) As Number                        ( s -- n )
Rem     The code of the first character. Aborts on the empty string,
Rem     which has none.

Rem Codes(s As String) As Array Of Number             ( s -- codes )
Rem     The code of every character, in order, as an ARRAY — so a scanner
Rem     indexes with Nth in O(1) rather than walking a list. An empty
Rem     string answers an empty array. Total.

Rem FromCodes(codes As Array Of Number) As String     ( codes -- s )
Rem     The string those codes spell. Codes' exact inverse, NUL included,
Rem     which is the one string Chr cannot build.

Rem CodeAt(s As String, k As Number) As Number        ( s k -- n )
Rem     The code of the character at position k. It REFUSES past the end
Rem     where Mid trims, and that is the point of it: Asc(Mid(s, k, 1))
Rem     reads the wrong character and then aborts naming Asc, a word away
Rem     from the index that was actually wrong.

Rem Str(x As Number) As String                        ( x -- s )
Rem     A number written out as text, the way the stack shows it — ten
Rem     significant digits, so an arbitrary double does not round-trip
Rem     bit-exactly.

Rem Val(s As String) As Number                        ( s -- x )
Rem     Text read back as a number, and the most dangerous builtin in
Rem     this file. It is strtod: it answers 0 for text it cannot read at
Rem     all, and takes a leading number off a longer token — so "twelve"
Rem     is 0 and "3kg" is 3, and neither says so. Ask IsNumeric first, or
Rem     reach for ValOr.

Rem ValOr(s As String, fallback As Number) As Number  ( s fallback -- x )
Rem     The same read, answering fallback for text that is not a number.
Rem     The total form of the pair.

Rem IsNumeric(s As String) As Boolean                 ( s -- flag )
Rem     Whether Val would read this text as a number — the question Val
Rem     itself does not ask. It means "would VAL succeed", not "is this
Rem     wholly a number", so "3kg" passes it.

Rem ---- this machine's own words --------------------------------------

Rem Split carries a position rather than a shrinking string. It used to
Rem recurse on Mid(s, k + Len(sep), Len(s)) — a fresh copy of the whole
Rem tail per field — because Instr can only search from the beginning, so
Rem splitting into n fields copied the subject n times and Split was
Rem quadratic. InstrFrom searches from a position without copying to it,
Rem which leaves only the field itself to copy: the same answers, in
Rem linear time, for the most-used word in the library.
Rem
Rem The refusal stays in Split and stays first, so Split(s, "") errors
Rem before any scanning happens, exactly as it did.
Def Split(s As String, sep As String) As List Of String
    If Len(sep) = 0 Then
        Error("SPLIT: EMPTY SEPARATOR")
    Else
        SplitFrom(s, sep, 1)

Def SplitFrom(s As String, sep As String, i As Number) As List Of String
    SplitAt(s, sep, i, InstrFrom(s, sep, i))

Rem A position past the end is not a mistake here: the separator ending
Rem the subject leaves an empty last field, and Mid answers "" for it.
Def SplitAt(s As String, sep As String, i As Number, k As Number) As List Of String
    If k = 0 Then
        { Mid(s, i, Len(s) - i + 1) }
    Else
        Prepend(Mid(s, i, k - i), SplitFrom(s, sep, k + Len(sep)))

Def Join(xs As List Of String, sep As String) As String
    If IsEmpty(xs) Then
        ""
    Else
        Fold(Rest(xs), First(xs), Fn(a, x) => a & sep & x)

Def Trim(s As String) As String
    If Len(s) = 0 Then
        s
    Else
        If Left(s, 1) = " " Then
            Trim(Mid(s, 2, Len(s)))
        Else
            If Right(s, 1) = " " Then
                Trim(Left(s, Len(s) - 1))
            Else
                s

Rem Replace gets the same treatment and for the same reason: it recursed
Rem on a copy of the tail, so every substitution copied the rest of the
Rem subject before searching it. Carrying the position removes that copy
Rem entirely.
Rem
Rem It does NOT make Replace linear, and Split's comment above should not
Rem be read as saying so. What remains is the & chain — each level joins
Rem its piece onto the whole result of the levels below it, which is
Rem quadratic in the number of substitutions and cannot be otherwise
Rem while & is how strings are assembled. Removing the tail copy roughly
Rem halves the work; making the assembly linear is a different change and
Rem would need a word this library does not have.
Def Replace(s As String, old As String, new As String) As String
    If Len(old) = 0 Then
        Error("REPLACE: EMPTY PATTERN")
    Else
        ReplaceFrom(s, old, new, 1)

Def ReplaceFrom(s As String, old As String, new As String, i As Number) As String
    ReplaceAt(s, old, new, i, InstrFrom(s, old, i))

Def ReplaceAt(s As String, old As String, new As String, i As Number, k As Number) As String
    If k = 0 Then
        Mid(s, i, Len(s) - i + 1)
    Else
        Mid(s, i, k - i) & new & ReplaceFrom(s, old, new, k + Len(old))

Def StartsWith(s As String, p As String) As Boolean
    Left(s, Len(p)) = p

Def EndsWith(s As String, p As String) As Boolean
    Right(s, Len(p)) = p

Def StrRep(s As String, n As Number) As String
    If n <= 0 Then
        ""
    Else
        s & StrRep(s, n - 1)

Rem ---- conversions -----------------------------------------------------
Rem The boolean side of the guard family, named to match the number side
Rem exactly: IsNumeric / Val / ValOr are builtins, and IsBool / ToBool /
Rem ToBoolOr do the same three offices for booleans. ToBool is strict —
Rem TRUE or FALSE, any case, outer space ignored — while ToBoolOr and
Rem ToBoolOpt also accept the prompt vocabulary: TRUE/T/YES/Y/1 and
Rem FALSE/F/NO/N/0.
Rem
Rem ToBoolOpt is the primitive and the other two are one-liners over it.
Rem That is the whole point of Option: a strict word throws the answer
Rem away on failure and a fallback word throws away the fact that there
Rem WAS a failure, so neither can be built from the other — but both are
Rem built from this. Option is the language's, not a machine's, so no
Rem Include is needed to match on what this returns.
Rem
Rem These were Bool and BoolOr until v1.7.0. BoolOr in particular read as
Rem a logic gate and was in fact a parser, and the Bool* prefix belonged
Rem to bit and boolean work rather than to string conversion.

Def IsBool(s As String) As Boolean
    Let u = Upper(Trim(s))
    u = "TRUE" Or u = "FALSE"

Def ToBoolOpt(s As String) As Option
    Let u = Upper(Trim(s))
    If u = "TRUE" Or u = "T" Or u = "YES" Or u = "Y" Or u = "1" Then
        Some(True)
    Else
        If u = "FALSE" Or u = "F" Or u = "NO" Or u = "N" Or u = "0" Then
            Some(False)
        Else
            None()

Def ToBool(s As String) As Boolean
    If IsBool(s) Then
        Upper(Trim(s)) = "TRUE"
    Else
        Error("TOBOOL: '" & s & "' is not TRUE or FALSE")

Def ToBoolOr(s As String, fallback As Boolean) As Boolean
    Select Case ToBoolOpt(s)
        Case Some(b)
            b
        Case None
            fallback

Rem ---- formatting ------------------------------------------------------
Rem Fixed-width fields for monospace readouts. Every mill was growing its
Rem own pads and decimal formatter; these are the library versions.

Rem s padded with spaces to width n; unchanged if already that wide.
Def PadLeft(s As String, n As Number) As String
    StrRep(" ", n - Len(s)) & s

Def PadRight(s As String, n As Number) As String
    s & StrRep(" ", n - Len(s))

Rem x as a whole number zero-padded to at least n digits ("07"); a minus
Rem sign rides outside the padding.
Def PadZero(x As Number, n As Number) As String
    If x < 0 Then
        "-" & PadZero(0 - x, n)
    Else
        Let d = Str(Round(x))
        StrRep("0", n - Len(d)) & d

Rem x with exactly p decimal places — "0.40", never "0.4" — rounded
Rem half-up at the last shown digit. p = 0 is the whole-number
Rem formatter: no decimal point.
Def ToFixed(x As Number, p As Number) As String
    If p <= 0 Then
        Str(Round(x))
    Else
        Let s = 10 ^ p
        Let n = Round(x * s)
        Let a = Abs(n)
        Ifte(n < 0, "-", "") & Str(Fix(a / s)) & "." & PadZero(a Mod s, p)

Rem A digit string grouped in threes from the right: "1234567" becomes
Rem "1,234,567". Commas' helper; digits only, no sign.
Def CommaGroup(digits As String) As String
    If Len(digits) <= 3 Then
        digits
    Else
        CommaGroup(Left(digits, Len(digits) - 3)) & "," & Right(digits, 3)

Rem The classic ##,###.## — x with thousands commas and p decimals.
Def Commas(x As Number, p As Number) As String
    Let q = Max(p, 0)
    Let s = 10 ^ q
    Let n = Round(x * s)
    Let a = Abs(n)
    Let head = Ifte(n < 0, "-", "") & CommaGroup(Str(Fix(a / s)))
    If q = 0 Then
        head
    Else
        head & "." & PadZero(a Mod s, q)
